8246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b63f5657-355a-5f0a-a214-2065b7a76d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125071 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\cdot 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $λ(λ + 2)(λ - 1) = 0$\n2. $λ^{3} + 3λ^{2} - 2λ + 4 = 0$\n3. $λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} - 10λ = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5a631d9-8e9c-5aa8-b5b9-8e752a0ab9bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449345 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449346 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1071 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449391 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449392 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449393 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449457 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449458 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449459 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- **II.** $A+2I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1061 20109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "babad562-3d88-57e5-a6c7-a8b53bec4956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1061 20111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46208d38-6fce-5121-8f5a-512e318a7731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1061 20113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4880d3b1-8971-5b5f-8646-d786ae6c89fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1061 31946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e492707b-f646-51d5-b4f4-34c984760081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1065 3569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e561ab8-38f8-5ec1-a285-6559f35aa7cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453066 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453067 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453068 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed9c37d8-c5de-5898-be3f-6a097e562da9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362245 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453075 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453076 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453077 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C-5I$ is a non-singular matrix.\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23a6bd8a-b3a8-5b1e-9289-48d8854d334a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362250 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453090 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453091 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453092 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- **II.** $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89915cc7-c25f-5711-a62f-10d7e3f1b8b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362256 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453093 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453094 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453095 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C+3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362257 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472400 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472401 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472402 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$\n- **II.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f94c3089-795c-58b5-a020-124bd7b98fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380325 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454887 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454888 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454889 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- **II.** $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a33206bb-7969-5524-a745-290fffeff16f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186448608 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/r